<RaiseFault name="RF-NaaSAdminRoleRequired">
  <DisplayName>403 — missing NaaS.Admin role</DisplayName>
  <!--
    The only endpoint on this platform where Apigee enforces a SECOND, finer
    role on a sub-path. Everywhere else one role gates a whole use-case
    prefix and the application decides the rest.

    Why the exception: POST /api/naas/v1/admin/tickets/{id}/approve approves a
    real Ethernet Port order — irreversible and commercially binding. Until
    this policy existed, the only thing standing between a NaaS user and that
    action was the ADMIN_ROLES value in naas-agent-api's /secrets/.env
    payload. That fails closed if empty (an empty role intersection raises
    403), but set WRONG — say to NaaS.User — every NaaS user silently becomes
    an approver and nothing upstream would notice.

    THE COST, STATED PLAINLY: this authorization decision now lives in two
    places — here, and ADMIN_ROLES in the backend's secret. They must be
    changed together. That duplication is normally the thing to avoid (see
    the note beside RF-NaaSRoleRequired in proxies/default.xml); it is
    accepted here only because of what the endpoint does.

    Platform.Admin is deliberately NOT accepted. It can reach NaaS at all
    (bff/app/proxy/routes.py and the frontend's ROLE_NAAS both allow it) but
    approving circuit orders is a narrower right than platform
    administration. Translation and Sales treat Platform.Admin as a universal
    pass, so NaaS diverges here on purpose — see docs/25.
  -->
  <FaultResponse>
    <Set>
      <StatusCode>403</StatusCode>
      <ReasonPhrase>Forbidden</ReasonPhrase>
      <Payload contentType="application/json">{"error":"missing_role","required":"NaaS.Admin"}</Payload>
    </Set>
  </FaultResponse>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</RaiseFault>
